    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Area Between Two Curves Expressed as Functions of X",
    "problem": "Calculate the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y=2{x}^{2}-2$ and $y=6.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/0fd8a89bdcad9f1b20c9fd35df9aa209a5e551d5d7a7c97f62103e23e7ae9741.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-2 & =6 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/83e8d6af5284006b4caad6b1a96cb1298f7c7672d6c35fa652359d3a0b28e037.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=2{x}^{2}-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=6.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(6)-(2{x}^{2}-2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(8-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =[8\\cdot 2-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3}]-[8\\cdot (-2)-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-2)^{3}] \\\\  & =(16-\\frac{16}{3})-(-16+\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
